WHY THIS IS LOAD-BEARING AND NOT BOOKKEEPING, measured rather than restated: `mcp-tool-allowlist.ts:94` computes
```
...[...OPERATOR_PRINCIPAL_KINDS].filter((kind) => !MCP_REACHABLE_OPERATOR_KINDS.has(kind)).sort(),
```
so `MCP_EXCLUDED_COMMAND_KINDS` is genuinely DERIVED from `OPERATOR_PRINCIPAL_KINDS`, exactly as the governor ruling said. That is the reason step 2 widened the fence's disjunct instead of editing the roster: removing `AGENT_PROVIDER_COMMAND_KIND` from `OPERATOR_PRINCIPAL_KINDS` would have dropped it out of the derived exclusion in the same line and made the kind MCP-REACHABLE — an agent arriving as the operator over the MCP bootstrap credential — while the diff looked like a one-word authorization change. My diff leaves `OPERATOR_PRINCIPAL_KINDS` untouched, so the derivation cannot have moved.

THE ARM (`keeps the kind MCP-unreachable in BOTH directions`), enumerating the SERVED set from the dispatch seam per global rail 9, not from the roster constant:
```ts
const served = new Set<string>(wiredMcpToolKinds());
const excluded = new Set<string>(MCP_EXCLUDED_COMMAND_KINDS);
expect(excluded.has(AGENT_PROVIDER_COMMAND_KIND)).toBe(true);
expect(served.has(AGENT_PROVIDER_COMMAND_KIND)).toBe(false);
expect([...served].filter((kind) => excluded.has(kind))).toEqual([]);
expect([...excluded].filter((kind) => served.has(kind))).toEqual([]);
expect(deps.registry.has(AGENT_PROVIDER_COMMAND_KIND)).toBe(true);
```
Both directions of the disjointness are asserted from BOTH sides, because a single-sided filter shrinks with whichever roster it iterates and stays green while a served capability quietly vanishes from the advertised surface — the exact defect global rail 9 was written from. The final line pins that the exclusion is MCP-ONLY: the registry still SERVES the kind, so "MCP-closed" cannot be satisfied by accidentally de-registering it.

